  "content": {
    "id": "p66-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Show that $(2x-1)$ is a factor of $P(x)=6x^3+23x^2-33x+10$.\nThen fully factorise $P(x)$ (by long division or comparing coefficients) and state the zeros.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P\\left(\\frac12\\right)=0$; $P(x)=(2x-1)(3x-2)(x+5)$. Zeros: $\\frac12,\\frac23,-5$.",
      "worked": "$$P\\left(\\frac12\\right)=6\\left(\\frac18\\right)+23\\left(\\frac14\\right)-33\\left(\\frac12\\right)+10=0.$$\nBy the factor theorem, $x-\\frac12$, and hence $2x-1$, is a factor.\nWrite $P(x)=(2x-1)(ax^2+bx+c)$. Expanding gives\n$$P(x)=2ax^3+(2b-a)x^2+(2c-b)x-c.$$\nEquating coefficients:\n$$\\begin{align*}2a&=6&&\\Rightarrow a=3\\\\2b-a&=23&&\\Rightarrow b=13\\\\-c&=10&&\\Rightarrow c=-10.\\end{align*}$$\nThese also satisfy $2c-b=-33$.\n$$P(x)=(2x-1)(3x^2+13x-10)=(2x-1)(3x-2)(x+5).$$\nBy the null factor law, the zeros are $x=\\frac12,\\frac23,-5$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 80
  },
